      "text": "To solve the equation \\(5 = \\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x}{x+2}\\), we first simplify the right-hand side by factoring the numerator. The numerator \\(x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x\\) can be factored by taking out the common factor \\(x\\):\n\n\\[x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x = x(x^2 - 2x - 8)\\]\n\nNext, we factor the quadratic expression \\(x^2 - 2x - 8\\):\n\n\\[x^2 - 2x - 8 = (x - 4)(x + 2)\\]\n\nSo, the equation becomes:\n\n\\[5 = \\frac{x(x - 4)(x + 2)}{x + 2}\\]\n\nFor \\(x \\neq -2\\), we can cancel the \\(x + 2\\) terms in the numerator and the denominator:\n\n\\[5 = x(x - 4)\\]\n\nThis simplifies to the quadratic equation:\n\n\\[5 = x^2 - 4x\\]\n\nRearranging terms, we get:\n\n\\[x^2 - 4x - 5 = 0\\]\n\nWe can factor this quadratic equation:\n\n\\[x^2 - 4x - 5 = (x - 5)(x + 1) = 0\\]\n\nSo, the solutions are:\n\n\\[x = 5 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = -1\\]\n\nWe need to check if these solutions are valid by ensuring they do not make the original denominator zero. The original equation has the denominator \\(x + 2\\). Substituting \\(x = 5\\) into the denominator, we get:\n\n\\[5 + 2 = 7 \\neq 0\\]\n\nSo, \\(x = 5\\) is a valid solution. Substituting \\(x = -1\\) into the denominator, we get:\n\n\\[-1 + 2 = 1 \\neq 0\\]\n\nSo, \\(x = -1\\) is also a valid solution. The sum of the valid \\(x\\)-values is:\n\n\\[5 + (-1) = 4\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the \\(x\\)-values that satisfy the equation is \\(\\boxed{4}\\).",
