      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the equation \\(5 = \\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x}{x + 2}\\), we first simplify the right-hand side. Notice that the numerator \\(x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x\\) can be factored by factoring out \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\nx^3 - 2x^2 - 8x = x(x^2 - 2x - 8)\n\\]\n\nNext, we factor the quadratic expression \\(x^2 - 2x - 8\\):\n\n\\[\nx^2 - 2x - 8 = (x - 4)(x + 2)\n\\]\n\nSo, the numerator becomes:\n\n\\[\nx(x - 4)(x + 2)\n\\]\n\nThus, the original equation can be rewritten as:\n\n\\[\n5 = \\frac{x(x - 4)(x + 2)}{x + 2}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(x \\neq -2\\), we can cancel the \\(x + 2\\) terms in the numerator and the denominator:\n\n\\[\n5 = x(x - 4)\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to the quadratic equation:\n\n\\[\nx^2 - 4x - 5 = 0\n\\]\n\nWe factor the quadratic equation:\n\n\\[\nx^2 - 4x - 5 = (x - 5)(x + 1) = 0\n\\]\n\nSo, the solutions are:\n\n\\[\nx = 5 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = -1\n\\]\n\nWe need to check if these solutions are valid by ensuring they do not make the original denominator zero. The original denominator is \\(x + 2\\). For \\(x = 5\\):\n\n\\[\n5 + 2 = 7 \\neq 0\n\\]\n\nFor \\(x = -1\\):\n\n\\[\n-1 + 2 = 1 \\neq 0\n\\]\n\nBoth solutions are valid. The sum of the solutions is:\n\n\\[\n5 + (-1) = 4\n\\]\n\nThus, the sum of the \\(x\\)-values that satisfy the equation is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}\n\\]",
